\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
mega2r
\emph default
 Marker Range Functions
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2-Package-Marker"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We might have genotype data for thousands of samples and millions of markers
 in a study.
 But we are unlikely to have the memory to support this large a matrix in
 R.
 The Mega2 genotype data is compressed in the SQLite database and remains
 compressed in its data frame.
 Most of the time, we do not need to decompress all of it at once.
 We typically want to examine the markers of a chromosome or of some genes
 of interest.
 We only decompress the set of markers we need.
 The key idea is we want to find the set of markers that fall in some base
 pair range and process just those markers.
 We will introduce two ways to specify these ranges.
 In addition, we want to iterate through multiple ranges as would appear
 in one or more gene transcripts and process the markers.
 Finally, we process a each range of markers by invoking a callback function
 on the range, markers and related data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal list of the chromosome and base pair ranges for many
 gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records of the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 The list contains about 29,000 records.
 But there are several reasons that default transcript ranges may not be
 to your liking.
 Sometimes the transcripts for a gene appear to have overlapping start/end
 position.
 The transcripts have one start and one end but may not account for non
 coding regions, promoters, etc.
 Perhaps you would prefer to specify a transcript names and for each name
 provide a start position, end position and chromosome.
 The 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
 function lets you store custom ranges; it has two arguments: a range data
 frame that has at least the start position, end position and chromosome
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

observations
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and a selector vector of 3 integer that indicates which observation variable
 in the range data frame contains the chromosome, start position and end
 position.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function (and later 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function) take an initial argument that is a callback function that is
 called repeatedly for each transcript that has markers present.
 They also take a final argument that is an 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.

\emph on
 applyFnToRanges,
\emph default
 with no additional arguments, will iterate through each row of the Mega2R
 ranges, determine the markers contained between the start and end, compute
 the genotype matrix, and then invoke a callback function.
 Alternatively, 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 may be given a range argument and a selector argument which will cause
 it to use the ranges supplied rather than the default.
 These two arguments are the same as provided to 
\emph on
setRanges
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 requires a call back function of four arguments: the first is a matrix
 of genotypes for all the markers in the transcript, the second is a data
 frame of the markers in the transcript, the third is a single row from
 the data frame that defines the transcript (minimally it has a name, ID,
 start position, end position, and chromosome), and the final argument is
 the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that contains all the Mega2R data frames.
 The call back function can apply whatever analysis is required and it can
 access the other data frames available in the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 and even store intermediate results back into the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
setAnnotations and applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R also loads a list of genes and their transcripts from the Bioconductor
 data Annotations: "org.Hs.eg.db"and "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene".
 The former translates a gene name or alias to the entrez ID of the gene.
 The latter gives start position, end position and chromosome for each transcrip
t known for the each gene.
 You might want to use the transcript data from another build that is available
 at Bioconductor or follow their instructions make your own mapping.
 The 
\emph on
mega2r
\emph default
 function, 
\emph on
setAnnotations
\emph default
, lets you supply alternate string names for these two tables.
 Mega2R will load the selected tables when you access the 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 has a call back function of four arguments just like 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
.
 You specify a list of genes to extract the transcripts for; then Mega2R
 computes the list of markers that belong to each transcript.
 Mega2R computes an array of genotypes from the markers of each transcript
 and then it invokes the call back function that is supplied as the first
 argument to the 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 function.
 The callback function was explained at the end of the previous section
 for 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 can take a number named arguments that specify other ways to indicate transcrip
t ranges: on sub-ranges of chromosomes (ranges_arg argument), on the entire
 chromosome (chrs_arg argument), and/or on an arbitrary list of markers
 (markers_args argument).
 The callback is be repeatedly applied to every range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 is the simplest function that uses the aforementioned callback function.
 It takes one argument that is rows of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 It creates a genotype matrix for these markers and then invokes the call
 back function with the genotype matrix, the marker argument data frame,
 NULL, and the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 (NULL because there is no range information.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection

\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 functions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The functions, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 and 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 return a matrix of nucleotide pairs or a matrix of encoded integers with
 a column for each marker and containing a row per sample.
 Both functions take a single argument which is rows of the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 data frame.
 This argument is computed by either the 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
, 
\emph on
applyFnToGenes
\emph default
 or 
\emph on
applyFnToMarkers
\emph default
 function.
 The
\series bold
 marker 
\series default
data frame has two offsets that are used internally by the decompression
 code; the 
\series bold
marker
\series default
 also has a name, chromosome number and position that can be used to identify
 a marker to the user.
 The two 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 functions are dispatches that call Rcpp code.
 They collect genotype and allele data from the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

R environment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 data frames and pass these data arguments to the Rcpp code; they also call
 the proper function for the level of compression: 2 bits or 2 bytes per
 marker.
 For each marker specified, 
\emph on
getgenotypes
\emph default
 will return for each sample the corresponding pair of nucleotides as a
 string, while 
\emph on
getgenotypesraw
\emph default
 will return the first allele integer value and the second allele integer
 value; the two integers are returned as a single integer with the integer
 for the first allele shifted 16 bits and or'ed to the integer for the second
 allele.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 Function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2pedgene-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'pedgene' package performs Gene-level association tests with disease
 status for pedigree data: kernel and burden association tests on family
 data, and is available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=pedgene).
 It was written by Daniel Schaid and Jason Sinnwell
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "pedgene"

\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
[Schaid DJ, McDonnell SK., Sinnwell JP, Thibodeau SN.
 (2013) Multiple Genetic Variant Association Testing by Collapsing and Kernel
 Methods With Pedigree or Population Structured Data, Genet Epidemiol, 37(5):409
-18.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 invokes 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 on the default ranges and serves as an example of the 
\emph on
applyFnToRanges
\emph default
 function.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We call 
\emph on
init_pedgene
\emph default
 rather than 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB
\emph default
; the former uses the function in the 
\emph on
mega2r
\emph default
 package to just load a Mega2 database (
\emph on
dbmega2_import
\emph default
).
 Then it generates a modified the 
\series bold
fam
\series default
 data frame; it must eliminate samples where the case/control is not known
 which is required by `pedgene`.
 In addition, 
\emph on
init_pedgene 
\emph default
computes and saves some state into the environment to be used later.
 The CRAN package, 
\emph on
pedgene,
\emph default
 requires an encoding of the genotypes 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 to 0, 1, and 2 respectiv
ely; plus the genotypes must be flipped so that 1 is the allele with the
 minor allele frequency.
 DOpedgene transforms its genotype input matrix and then it calls the 
\emph on
pedgene
\emph default
 function with several different marker weights.
 It returns the p-value of the kernel and burden association statistics
 for each weight.
 The results are stored in a data frame.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The call back function, 
\emph on
DOpedgene
\emph default
, duplicates the code that we used in an earlier 
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

manual run
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 We had the results of running `pedgene` on many transcript ranges for one
 of our private dataset and we got the same results running 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene
\emph default
 on the same dataset.
 But the original code harness was hand coded and hard to change.
\end_layout

