\begin_layout Standard
The code in each function illustrates how to assemble the corresponding
 data from the Mega2R data frames.
 The 
\emph on
mkVCFphe
\emph default
 is a bit subtle.
 The database contains a raw vector of 8 bytes * the number of phenotypes
 (per person).
 The 8 bytes are either one double precision float number or two integers
 depending on the encoding in the 
\series bold
locus_table
\series default
 (quantitative or affection status).
 
\emph on
readBin
\emph default
 is used to convert the raw bytes in each 8 byte vector to the correct form.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Another interesting aspect of the code design is how we compute genotype
 data for a large numbers of markers without needing large amount of memory.
 We build the .
\series bold
vcf
\series default
 file a chunk at a time, where a chunk contains a relatively small number
 of markers (currently 1000).
 We generate the genotype matrix for each chunk then append the results
 for the chunk (of markers) to the .vcf file.
 Then, we get a new chunk and repeat.
 This looping strategy might prove useful for other situations where the
 results can be written incrementally.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection

\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 Function 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:mega2genabel-Package-Functions"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'GenABEL'
\begin_inset CommandInset citation
LatexCommand cite
key "genabel"

\end_inset

 package is available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=GenABEL).
 The reference is [Aulchenko Y.S., Ripke S., Isaacs A., van Duijn C.M.
 GenABEL: an R package for genome-wide association analysis.
 Bioinformatics.
 2007 23(10):1294-6.] The GenABEL CRAN package provides many functions for
 GWAS analysis and it accepts GWAS data in several formats.
 But Mega2 accepts input in still more formats, notably, PLINK bed, VCF,
 IMPUTE2 and even linkage.
 Thus 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 can be a bridge to easily convert data for GenABEL analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You must call the 
\emph on
read.Mega2DB 
\emph default
function from the 
\emph on
mega2r
\emph default
 package to load your database and should save the returned `environment`
 into `ENV.` The function, 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
, returns a gwaa.data-class object for the selected markers.
 It operates by re-writing the Mega2R data frames into one of the formats
 that GenABEL supports; currently, this is PLINK .tped format.
 This requires a .tped file, a .fam file, and a .phe file.
 The .tped file looks very much like a VCF .vcf file with entries in each
 row for the samples' genotype data for each marker.
 (But, in comparison to the .vcf file, there are fewer fields of genotype
 metadata in a .tped file and the genotype data specify real nucleotide labels
 rather than VCF's REF/ALT field references.) 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 calls the GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

glue
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 functions,
\emph on
 convert.snp.tped
\emph default
, to process the a .tped file and .fam file into a generic GenABEL 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

raw
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 format file and then it converts that raw format file and a phenotype file
 into a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object via the function, 
\emph on
load.gwaa.data
\emph default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function 
\emph on
Mega2ENVGenABEL
\emph default
 is experimental but should produce same object as 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
.
 It does not write out files, but rather converts the Mega2R genotype and
 related data in memory to a gwaa.data-class object.
 It is mainly written in Rcpp and runs 10 to 20 times faster than the 
\emph on
Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
 function.
 A bit more testing and we will suggest 
\emph on
Mega2ENVGenABEL
\emph default
 be used exclusively.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The rest of this document shows some complete examples of Mega2R package
 use.
 First, we illustrate the base functions of the package, 
\emph on
mega2
\emph default
r.
 Then we demonstrate several extended functions, 
\emph on
Mega2pedgene, Mega2VCF and Mega2GenABEL
\emph default
.
 Note: Not all the arguments and details of the functions are discussed
 in these examples, if you need programming details, look at the 'mega2r'
 CRAN package documentation or the actual code in the git repository.
\end_layout

