\begin_layout Subsubsection
Reading and Examining a Mega2 Database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The 'mega2r' package facilitates reading genetic data from a Mega2-created
 'SQLite' database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Reading a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After you have created the 'SQLite' database, start up the R program.
 Load the 'mega2r' package, then use the function `read.Mega2DB` to read
 a Mega2 database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimrdb,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(mega2r)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first argument should be the path to the database.
 Providing the optional argument, `verbose`, causes the read function to
 summarize the data frames created, their fields and their sizes.
 Finally, an `environment`, that contains the data frames is returned.
 (
\color violet
If you are unfamiliar with environments, you can think of them as data frames.
 `ENV$locus_table` will access the `locus_table` variable from `ENV` similar
 to fetching an "observation" from a data frame.
 The difference is when you change a data frame passed to a function, the
 change does not affect the original data frame.
 Only the function's local value is changed; ALL changes are forgotten when
 the function exits.
 If you change the data in an environment passed to a function, the change
 is permanent.
\color inherit
)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimrdb2>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db", verbose = TRUE)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For each table generated, if 'verbose' is TRUE, we emit two lines: one with
 the number of rows and number of columns of the data frame and the other
 with the column names of the data frame.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We need to make two observations that apply to all Mega2R functions:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Verbose Flag
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
When verbose is set in the initial read.Mega2DB, the value will be remembered.
 It may be used by any subsequent function.
 If verbose is TRUE, Mega2R functions can print diagnostic information.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Use of an Environment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
All Mega2R functions that do not return an environment need to have an environme
nt supplied as an argument.
 As stated earlier, the environment is used to store the data frames that
 contain the 'SQLite' database.
 There are two ways to pass the environment.
 If you assigned the result of `read.Mega2DB` to the variable `seqsimr`,
 then you could supply the value 'seqsimr' to any Mega2R function as the
 named argument `envir`:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\color violet
showMega2ENV(envir = seqsimr)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The second choice is a bit of a "hack" but it is very convenient.
 Every Mega2R function (that does not return an environment) has a named
 `envir` argument defined to take on the default value `ENV`: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\color violet
envir = ENV
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
as in
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\color violet
showMega2ENV = function(envir = ENV) { ...
 }
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The code above, assigns global variable `ENV` to the local variable, `envir`.
 Thus if `envir` is not provided in the function call, R will look up the
 value of `ENV` in the global environment.
 Hopefully, `ENV` was set to the return value of `read.Mega2DB` 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Back to more examples.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The `ls` function will show you all the variables in an environment.
 (You probably have used it without arguments to show you the variables
 in the .GlobalEnv.) Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<ls>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ls(ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A more informative overview of the database can be had with:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<showMENV>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

showMega2ENV() 
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code

\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Use standard R operations to examine the created data frames 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Try typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<str>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

str(ENV$locus_table)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Iterating through Gene Transcripts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are two ways to compute a function on all the genotypes (or markers)
 in a transcript.
 These examples are explained more thoroughly in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:pedgene"

\end_inset

 below.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
setRanges 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "par:applyFnToRanges"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for many gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records from the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 The list contains about 29,000 records.
 You may load your own range set instead of the default.
 You create a data frame that contains at least a name "observation", a
 chromosome "observation", a start position "observation" and an end position
 "observation".
 And you create an integer vector that contains the column numbers of the
 chromosome "observation", the start position "observation" and the end
 position "observation".
 These two become the arguments to the `setRanges` function, viz.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setRanges(Ranges, Columns)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToRanges
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, envir = ENV) 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
goes through each transcript/range entry and finds the markers that fall
 within the bounds.
 It then builds a genotype matrix for the samples and each marker in the
 range.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The first argument of the `applyFnToRanges` function is a call back function.
 The function is called with four arguments: the genotype matrix, the markers
 in range, the selected transcript/range entry and the environment.
 The call back is invoked repeatedly for each transcript range that contains
 any markers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If it is necessary to store information between successive invocations the
 environment (`envir`) can be used.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
`applyFnToRanges` can also be provided explicit ranges.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyF2R,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges, ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges[ENV$refRanges$chromosome ==1,],
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

       ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
setAnnotations
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Alternatively, you may search by genes.
 Here, the default transcript database is "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene"
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it, by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations("txdb", "genedb")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where "txdb" is a string that is the name of transcript database that was
 fetched from Bioconductor, and similarly "genedb" is the name of a Bioconductor
 database that maps a gene id from the transcript to an entrez gene id.
 The `setAnnotations` command must be issued whenever R is started and the
 `mega2r` library has been loaded.
 By way of a reminder, Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Bioconductor"

\end_inset

 explains how to choose and install "TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene" or a
 different Annotation database.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: We could require that you add to your session
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
library(<name of the transcription database>)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
but we have chosen not to.
 Instead, you just indicate the database name and the logic of `applyFnToGenes`
 does a `loadNamespace` of the name string and then a `get` of the name
 string from the namespace.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
applyFnToGenes 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyF2G,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'KIF26B', 'AK5', 'ST7L'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
will find all the transcripts for each gene requested from the Bioconductor
 database.
 It will then behave like `applyFnToRanges`: find all the markers in each
 transcript range, compute the genotype matrix and then invoke the call
 back function with the required four arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The `applyFnToGenes` function has several other optional arguments that
 can request complete chromosomes, (multiple) ranges of base pairs on chromosome
s, or collections of markers in addition to the genes_arg argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Iterating through the Genotypes 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Iterating-through-the"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Encoded Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The heart of the above two functions is the calculation of the genotype
 matrix of samples by markers containing the nucleotides (strings).
 The genotype information is most often stored in a compressed 2 bit representat
ion.
 An Rcpp function does the conversion of the compressed genotype data to
 nucleotides.
 The function
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
getgenotypes(markers, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
returns the matrix for the specified markers.
 It can take one additional argument that supplies a string to separate
 the two alleles of each marker.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Paragraph
Raw Genotypes
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The function
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
getgenotypesraw(markers, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
is similar to the `getgenotypes` function except that the matrix it returns
 contains an integer encoding for each genotype.
 The integer's high 16 bits are the index for allele1 and the low 16 bits
 are the index for allele2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: There are actually two different Rcpp functions for each named function
 in Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Iterating-through-the"

\end_inset

 One function processes compressed genotype data and the other function
 processes uncompressed genotype data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:pedgene"

\end_inset

 Using Mega2R to carry out automated gene-based association tests using
 'pedgene' 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides functions that permit one to run the 'pedgene' package to
 carry out gene-based association tests on 
\color black
family data looping over selected marker subsets.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The 'pedgene' package implements methods for carrying out gene-based association
 tests on family data, and is
\color inherit
 
\color violet
available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=pedgene).
 It was written by Daniel Schaid and Jason Sinnwell: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
[Schaid DJ, McDonnell SK., Sinnwell JP, Thibodeau SN.
 (2013) Multiple Genetic Variant Association Testing by Collapsing and Kernel
 Methods With Pedigree or Population Structured Data, Genet Epidemiol, 37(5):409
-18.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Gene transcripts reminder
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you plan to select transcripts by gene name, you must load them from
 Bioconductor.
 In Section 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "subsec:Bioconductor"

\end_inset

, we indicated that you needed to type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<bioc2,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

source("https://bioconductor.org/biocLite.R")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

biocLite("TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene") 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

biocLite("org.Hs.eg.db")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
just once to install the package.
 And then, to use the desired transcription data base, use this command
 from the 'mega2r' package as part of your session:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations(txdb, entrezGene)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where `txdb` is the name of Bioconductor transcription database, and `entrezGene
` is the name of Bioconductor mapping of gene name or gene alias to entrez
 gene id.
 Note: You do not need this command if you are using the default values
 shown above: TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene and org.Hs.eg.db.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Loading a Mega2 database
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Rather than read the Mega2 'SQLite' database with the `read.Mega2DB` function,
 described previously, here we use a specialized `init_pedgene` function
 to read the Mega2 database.
 This latter function calls a utility function also used by `read.Mega2DB`.
 Then it creates, edits, and rewrites the family data, storing it in a `pedgene`
-compatible data frame, `fam` .
 (`fam` merges data from the `pedigree_table`, `person_table` and `phenotype_tab
le`.) `init_pedgene` purges persons with unknown case/control status which
 is necessary for the pedgene calculation.
 (When `fam` is updated, similar filtering is done to the persons in the
 phenotype_table and the genotype_table.) Finally, `init_pedgene` calculates
 some values that will be used repeatedly and stores them in the environment
 that is returned.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<init_pedgene,message=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = init_pedgene("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on transcripts
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R has an internal default list of the chromosome and base pair ranges
 for a number of gene transcripts.
 These transcripts come from the UCSC Genome Browser reference assembly
 GRCH37.
 The list was further modified to eliminate multiple records of the same
 gene with the exact same transcript start and transcript end.
 These data contain about 29,000 records.
 You may load your own range set instead of the default.
 You create a data frame that contains at least a name "observation", a
 chromosome "observation", a start position "observation" and an end position
 "observation".
 Then, you create an integer vector that contains the column numbers of
 the chromosome "observation", the start position "observation" and the
 end position "observation".
 These two objects are then arguments to the `setRanges` function, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setRanges(Transcripts, Columns)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
By default, the function `Mega2pedgene` examines the first 100 transcripts
 and prints the results.
 (Internally, it calls the function `applyFnToRanges`.) For the `seqsimr.db`
 database, the first 100 transcripts finds only one transcript with several
 markers.
 (It is found because it is on chromosome 1 and it has a range that overlaps
 with some of the markers in our study.) To make this tutorial exercise run
 faster, we noticed that the identified transcript appeared at transcript
 54; so we will restrict `Mega2pedgene` to a small range of transcripts
 around 54, viz.
 50 through 60.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: `verbose` needs to be TRUE, for the diagnostics to be printed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<Mega2pedgene>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV$verbose=TRUE
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2pedgene(gs=50:60)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You will see many reports of "No markers in range", because the database
 only contains markers on a subrange of chromosome 1 whereas the transcripts
 span the entire genome.
 Occasionally you will see a listing of a gene name, markers, and count
 of 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 genotypes, viz.
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24037 520 646 214 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24039 940 386 54 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24041 1377 3 0 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24048 860 460 60 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24494 789 501 90 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24499 891 442 47 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24506 891 442 47 
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
CEP104 snp24507 789 501 90
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The genotype matrix for these markers, along with the markers, the range
 used, and the environment are passed to the call back function `DOpedgene`.
 `DOpedgene` converts the genotype nucleotides 1/1, 1/2 and 2/2 to the values
 0, 1 and 2 (or 2, 1, 0 if 1/1 is the genotype for the allele with the minor
 allele frequency).
 Then it runs 'pedgene'.
 The results are automatically stored in a data frame with "observations":
 prefix of chromosome, gene, number of markers and base pair range followed
 by 'pedgene' data: kernel and burden, value and p-values, four values for
 each of three weightings of the markers.
 These data are saved in the data frame, `pedgene_results`, in the environment.
 They are also printed when `verbose` is TRUE.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The results are always appended to the `pedgene_results` data frame.
 You should truncate it when necessary.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You could run `Mega2pedgene` on all the transcript entries, but it takes
 a rather long time.
 You would type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<FnToRanges,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# we will skip this line for the document production because it takes too
 long
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToRanges(DOpedgene, ENV$refRanges, ENV$refIndices, envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you run the above test, you will see that genes DISP1 and KIF26B have
 at least one p-value less than 0.01 and AK5 and STL7 at least one less than
 0.03.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Running pedgene on selected genes 
\begin_inset CommandInset label
LatexCommand label
name "subsec:Running-pedgene-on"

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You may try searching for transcripts of specific genes.
 Here, the default transcript database is `TxDb.Hsapiens.UCSC.hg19.knownGene`
 from Bioconductor.
 Of course you can change it.
 Type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout LyX-Code
setAnnotations("txdb", "genedb")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where "txdb" is a string that is the name of transcript database that was
 fetched from Bioconductor, and similarly "genedb" is the name of a Bioconductor
 database that maps a gene id from the input to an entrez gene id.
 You need to install any new database with biocLite, as shown earlier.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We leave the command below as an exercise.
 It runs a bit slowly.
 It needs to find all the transcripts for each gene, to find all the markers
 between each pair of transcript start/end ranges, to compute the genotype
 matrix for these markers and finally to call the callback function with
 the appropriate arguments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyF2G2,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'KIF26B', 'AK5', 'ST7L'),
 envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But let us run this function for a few genes:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<applyFnToGenes,eval=TRUE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

applyFnToGenes(DOpedgene, genes_arg = c('DISP1', 'AK5'), envir = ENV)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The default behavior of `DOpedgene` is to append any new results to
 the end of the `pedgene_results` data frame in the environment `envir`.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: You will observe that for several genes there are a few transcripts
 with the same or overlapping start values and end values.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Outputting Mega2R data to VCF format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The `Mega2VCF` function can output subsets of the mega2r database as a VCF
 file, accompanied by related files (for phenotype and family data).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The VCF data format (http://www.internationalgenome.org/wiki/Analysis/Variant%20Ca
ll%20Format/vcf-variant-call-format-version-40/) was originally defined
 by the 1000 Genomes Project (http://www.internationalgenome.org/home) for
 data storage.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The current version of data format can be found at (http://samtools.github.io/hts-
specs/).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Writing a VCF File using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First, create the directory "vcfr", so we can keep all the generated data
 in one place.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<mega2vcflib>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

if (! dir.exists("vcfr")) dir.create("vcfr")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The Mega2VCF function takes an argument which is the prefix used on all
 generated files.
 We will make it a path that includes the directory 
\series bold
vcfr
\series default
.
 Below, we assume that a Mega2 database was stored in the environment, `ENV`.
 If this is not the case, supply to Mega2VCF a named argument, `envir`,
 set to the environment you wish to use.
 (You will also have to change the references to the `markers` data frame,
 below, from `ENV`$markers to `<environment name>`$markers.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note that Mega2VCF will create the .vcf file as well as a .fam file (in linkage
 format), a .phe file (phenotypes) and a few others.
 These files are listed below.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Creating the VCF file and related files is accomplished by typing:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<mega2vcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

Mega2VCF("vcfr/vcf.01", ENV$markers[ENV$markers$chromosome==1 ,])
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: The line above places the data for only chromosome 1 in the files
 (Recall that our simulated data only contains chromosome 1).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note: In general, you can filter `ENV$markers` as needed.
 If the second argument is not present, all the markers are written out.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The generated files are listed below:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<lsvcf>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

list.files("vcfr")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
[This is redundant and could be deleted:] You can read the code to see how
 these files are created from the data frames.
 It is worth noting that the internal logic does not create a genotype matrix
 for all the requested markers at once, but rather works on a block of markers
 at a time, limiting the amount of memory required.
 The code also illustrates how to extract the phenotype values from the
 phenotype raw vectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Converting Mega2R data to GenABEL format
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Mega2R provides support for converting the data frames into 'GenABEL' format
 (e.g.
 as gwaa.data-class objects).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\color violet
The 'GenABEL' package is available on CRAN as (https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=
GenABEL).
 The reference is [Aulchenko Y.S., Ripke S., Isaacs A., van Duijn C.M.
 GenABEL: an R package for genome-wide association analysis.
 Bioinformatics.
 2007 23(10):1294-6.]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Creating a GenABEL gwaa.data-class object using Mega2R
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There are several data formats that Mega2 can read and transform into a
 database that are not directly accepted by GenABEL.
 But any Mega2 database that can be read into R, can be transformed to GenABEL
 following this example using 
\series bold
seqsimr.db
\series default
.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
GenABEL (http://www.genabel.org/) can process PLINK .tped/.tfam files with the
 function `convert.snp.tped` to create a GenABEL "raw" file.
 Then the "raw" file and a generated phenotype file can be processed by
 the function, `load.gwaa.data`, to yield a gwaa.data-class object.
 Currently, the function, `Mega2GenABEL`, uses this mechanism to convert
 Mega2 data frames to a gwaa.data-class object.
 The argument to `Mega2GenABEL` is a file name prefix that will be used
 to name the .tped/.fam/.phe files that are created.
 The GenABEL functions `convert.snp.tped` and `load.gwaa.data` are explained
 in https://cran.r-project.org/package=GenABEL.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If you have not done so already, install the 'GenABEL' package from CRAN.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then type:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<seqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

ENV = read.Mega2DB("seqsimr.db")
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# This line converts the database to a gwaa.data-class object.
 The intermediate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

# files begin with "SEQ"
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

seqsimgwaa = Mega2GenABEL("SEQ")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The files generated are:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<lsseq>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

list.files(pattern = "SEQ.*")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can use any of the GenABEL provided functions on the results, `seqsimgwaa`.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<strseqsimgwaa>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

str(seqsimgwaa)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

