\begin_layout Subsubsection
GenABEL regression
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This test is a bit more complicated.
 We intend to verify that the object generated by `Mega2GenABEL` is the
 same as the GenABEL object we started with.
 First, we load `GenABEL` and access its data:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<srdta>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

library(GenABEL)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

data(srdta)
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Then we dump the GenABEL data as PLINK .ped/.map/.phe files
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<dmpPed>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

dmpPed(gwaa_ = srdta, pfx = "srdta")
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To produce the Mega2 database `srdta.db`, we switch to the Unix command line
 and run:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Flex Chunk
status open

\begin_layout Plain Layout

<<unix2,eval=FALSE>>=
\end_layout

\begin_layout Plain Layout

mega2 MEGA2.BATCH.srdta
\end_layout

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Note: To make this tutorial only dependent on R, the above code is not actually
 run.
 And its results, shown below, were captured from an environment where we
 had both R and the Mega2 executable available.
\end_layout

